C COLLECTIONS LIBRARY - MAN PAGE TREE
======================================

This directory holds the real troff/groff manual pages for the library. There
is one file per documented symbol, with one exception: a family of related
constants shares a single page named after the family.


What gets a page
----------------

A symbol gets a page only if someone needs it in order to USE the library;
that is the whole test. Being declared in an installed header does not
qualify a symbol, and neither does a ccol_ prefix, being exported from the
shared object, or being a static inline function that a consumer could
technically call.

Before you add a page, name the user-facing task that cannot be done without
that symbol. If there is no such task, the symbol is internal: it gets no
page, no section in README.md or in a guide under doc/, and no SEE ALSO
entry. You may still mention it in the prose of another page to explain what
that page's symbol does.

A name that starts with an underscore is internal, and the underscore is
there precisely to say so at every call site. Such a name never gets a page.
An internal symbol may still live in an installed header and still be
exported, because a header cannot hide what the library itself needs across
translation units. It is the underscore, not the header or the symbol table,
that marks the boundary.

By that test, the following are internal, and none of them has a page:

    _csort_default_<type>_comparison_proc
        Every caller reaches these through csort_get_default_comparison_proc,
        which picks one from the type of its argument.

    _CCOL_CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
        The size arithmetic that CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER
        performs on the caller's behalf.

    _ccol_find_nearest_gte_power_of_two, ccol_strdup, ccol_growbuf_*
        Helpers that the containers use for their own growth and storage.

A literal constant that only matters as an argument, a buffer size or a field
value of some function never gets a page of its own, even when callers type
its name. Document it, together with the value it expands to, on the page of
each function or macro that takes it. A buffer length such as
CHTTPSVR_PEER_CERT_SHA256_LEN belongs on the page of the function that fills
the buffer, and a sentinel such as CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE belongs wherever the
field that accepts it is described (chttpsvr_start(3)). A set of constants
that many unrelated functions share, such as the HTTP status codes of
CHTTP_STATUS(3), keeps one page of its own so that the table is written only
once.

Layout
------

    man/
      <module>/<symbol>.3   one page per raw C function or type-inferred macro
      <module>/<module>.7   the module overview page, where there is one. It
                            holds the module-wide rules that do not fit on
                            any single page
      common/ccollections.7 the overview of the whole library, with the
                            conventions that all modules share. It is named
                            after the library rather than after common.h, so
                            it installs under a name of its own

<module> is the name of the header without its .h suffix, for example
cvector, chashmap or cjson. The raw functions and their companion
type-inferred macros live together in the same module directory; their names
never collide, so the file system needs no rule to keep them apart. The ".TH"
line of each page still records whether it documents a function or a macro:
its fourth field reads either "C Collections Library - Functions" or "C
Collections Library - Macros". In both cases the SYNOPSIS shows the real
declaration, and the SYNOPSIS and EXAMPLES name each header the way an
application includes it from an install, as <ccollections/NAME.h>.

Some symbols have more than one name, such as cjson_parse_mp and its
cjson_parse wrapper. Such a symbol gets one page with the content plus one or
more small ".so" redirect pages, which is how the glibc and BSD man pages
handle aliases.

A redirect page contains exactly one line, ".so <module>/<symbol>.3", and
nothing else: no ".TH" line and no comment. man then renders the target page
with that page's header and footer, and mandb records the alias as a link to
the target. The NAME section of the target lists every alias, so that whatis
and apropos can find each one.

A family of related constants is handled differently. The CHTTP_STATUS_*
constants of chttp.h are one such family: they carry no behavior of their
own, and a reader wants to see the whole table at once. They share a single
page, man/chttp/CHTTP_STATUS.3, whose NAME section lists every constant so
that apropos and whatis can find each one. There are no redirect pages for
the individual names, so "man CHTTP_STATUS" opens the table, but "man
CHTTP_STATUS_OK" does not resolve.

Viewing a page
--------------

`make install` copies every page from here into a real man3 and man7 tree
(see the root Makefile). After that, a plain `man cvector_create` works from
any directory, as does every alias. The one exception is a constant that
shares a family page, which you reach through the name of that page.

You can also read a page without installing anything. Run man from inside
this directory, so that the ".so <module>/<symbol>.3" redirect of an alias
page (described above) resolves correctly: "man -l" resolves a ".so" target
relative to the current working directory, not relative to the location of
the page.

    cd man && man -l cvector/cvector_create.3
    cd man && man -l cjson/cjson_parse.3   # an alias page, .so and all

Maintenance
-----------

When the documented behavior of a function or macro changes, edit the .3
file for that symbol directly, and keep the section order used by the
pages around it: NAME, LIBRARY, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, PARAMETERS, RETURN
VALUE, NOTES, WARNINGS, THREAD SAFETY, EXAMPLES, SEE ALSO. Write every new
page in plain ASCII.
